WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.950
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.530
So in this lecture,
we are going to start

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.915
looking at an example
of the QED process,

00:00:16.915 --> 00:00:19.290
for which we can now, with
all the tools we have in hand,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:23.290
calculate the matrix elements'
transition amplitude.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:24.310
All right.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.580
In more general terms, we
can look at all the examples.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.380
And they are listed here--

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.650
second-order processes and
one third-order process.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:36.310
We are going to discuss them
in more detail as we go along.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.800
This is really just to
give you some feedback

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.480
for the different
kinds of processes

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.140
we're going to look at.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.920
So the first one is
elastic scattering.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.420
And muon-electron
scattering, that's

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:49.300
the one process we're going
to look in more detail.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.080
Why?

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.300
Because this is
the simplest case.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.820
For this process, there is
only one leading-order diagram,

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:59.360
which is exactly
the one shown here.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.650
For other processes where have
the same particle interacting,

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:07.760
we find that we do have to
consider multiple diagrams--

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.310
for example, this one here,
where we have electron

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:11.840
and electron scattering.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.770
And so we have to calculate
not just this leading diagram,

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.100
which looks exactly like the
one for the muon scattering,

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.020
but we also have to
include the [INAUDIBLE]

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.660
where we change the
outgoing electron leg.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:27.850
And so on.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:29.970
And other processes
are including

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.700
electron-positron
scattering, which

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:36.060
is caused Bhabha scattering,
Compton scattering, which

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:39.080
we discussed the
kinematics for already,

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.130
but also inelastic processes
like pair annihilation or pair

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.520
production.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.085
There's a very
interesting diagram here,

00:01:47.085 --> 00:01:48.710
which is the third-order
diagram, which

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:51.280
is responsible for the
anomalous magnetic moment.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.840
And we'll talk more
about that when

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.470
we talk about
higher-order interactions.

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:59.320
So let's have a look at this
electron-muon scattering

00:01:59.320 --> 00:01:59.990
process.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:03.400
So only one diagram contributes
at the second order.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.580
And so you have an electron
and a muon scattering

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.440
via the exchange of a photon.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:11.620
This is after all
of QED diagram.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.610
So now, how do we calculate
the matrix element?

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.350
We simply just follow
the Feynman rules--

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:20.320
Feynman rules as we
discussed them before.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.680
And if you want to do this now,
you draw your Feynman diagram.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.760
It's always very good and useful
to draw a Feynman diagram first

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.710
and label accordingly.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:33.580
That's super-useful if you
want to systematically evaluate

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:34.960
this process.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.970
And then you start going
backwards from an outgoing leg

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:41.890
back to the initial leg.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.690
And you see this part here.

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:48.750
You have the u3, the
third particle here,

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:53.040
the vertex vector, and
the first particle.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.110
Then you have a propagator
here for your photon.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:01.680
It's given by minus i g
mu,nu divided by q square.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.530
And then you analyze
the second part here.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.710
Here you find the first
particle, vertex vector,

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:10.500
and the second particle.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:12.750
For each of those
lines, you have

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.760
to make sure that
energy and momentum is

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.490
converted into those
[INAUDIBLE] delta functions.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.190
And then the last
part you have to do,

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.230
integrate over your momentum.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:23.972
All right.

00:03:23.972 --> 00:03:24.930
That's already the end.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.500
The next step in
your list of rules

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:30.990
is carry out the integration.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.640
Integrate over q.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:34.445
That drops your delta
function, but you

00:03:34.445 --> 00:03:36.570
are left with one delta
function which you are also

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:42.630
supposed to drop, which then
gives you your matrix element.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.070
Now, here we're already done.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:47.580
If you now further want
to evaluate this diagram,

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.600
you actually have to be more
explicit about the spinors

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:52.660
involved.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.760
What needs to be done
now is have a discussion

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.560
on how to handle the
spin of the particles,

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.160
meaning being explicit
about the spinors.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.140
And in order to do
that, we'll discuss

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.390
how we treat spin, how
we have to treat spin,

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:06.150
either in an experiment
where the spin

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:09.210
of the initial particle is
known or an experiment where

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:12.240
we have to average over
all possible spin states.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.390
So that's part of the
next lecture discussion.

